  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths such that each of the 24 vertices in a regular 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one segment.\n\nFirst, let's consider the structure of a regular 24-gon. It has 24 vertices, and we need to draw 12 segments connecting these vertices. Each segment will connect two vertices, and since there are 24 vertices, we will have 12 segments because each segment connects two vertices.\n\nThe problem is equivalent to finding the number of ways to partition the 24 vertices into two sets of 12 vertices each, where each set forms a path in the 24-gon.\n\nIn a regular 24-gon, each vertex is connected to its immediate neighbors. If we draw a segment from one vertex to its neighbor, we can continue drawing segments in a circular manner until we return to the starting vertex. This forms a path that visits each vertex exactly once.\n\nSince the 24-gon is regular, each vertex has exactly two neighbors, and we can start from any vertex. The number of ways to partition the vertices into two sets of 12 vertices each is the same as the number of ways to start the path from any vertex and continue until we return to the starting vertex.\n\nThe number of ways to partition the 24 vertices into two sets of 12 vertices each is simply the number of ways to choose 12 vertices out of 24, which is given by the binomial coefficient:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{24}{12} = \\frac{24!}{12! \\cdot 12!}\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the 24-gon is regular and we are interested in the number of distinct paths, we need to consider the circular nature of the 24-gon. Each path can be rotated in 24 different ways, and each rotation of a path is considered the same. Therefore, we need to divide the number of ways to partition the vertices into two sets by 24 to account for these rotations.\n\nThus, the number of distinct ways to draw the 12 segments is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\binom{24}{12}}{24} = \\frac{24!}{12! \\cdot 12! \\cdot 24} = \\frac{24!}{12! \\cdot 12! \\cdot 24}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this value:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24!}{12! \\cdot 12! \\cdot 24} = \\frac{6204484017332394393600000000000000}{479001600 \\cdot 479001600 \\cdot 24} = 40425600\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths so that each vertex in the 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{40425600}\n\\]",
